# reshape.gen -- the composer changes the instrument, not the notes.
#
# Everything up to here composes what is played. This piece composes
# what it is played ON. Two chains emit no pitches at all: one rebuilds
# a channel around a different instrument every forty seconds, and the
# other reaches inside whatever is there and moves a number the patch
# never offered.
#
# UNIFICATION.md phase 4. The mechanism is the one the plan named before
# any of it was written: a composer emits an *intent* and the host
# carries it out. A plugin cannot link libthink and never touches a
# graph -- `gen::swap' knows nothing about `bell' except that the piece
# declared something by that name, handed to it already resolved the way
# a scale arrives as numbers. What it emits is "this channel becomes
# that instrument, at that time", and the host does it through the same
# patch-load path a person clicking in the Patch Selector uses.
#
# WHY IT IS AN EVENT AND NOT A FUNCTION CALL. Because everything else is.
# A structure edit is scheduled, so it happens on the transport rather
# than whenever a plugin felt like it; sparse, so nothing can thrash a
# channel; replayed from the seed, so this file sounds the same twice;
# and drawn on the roll, so you can watch one coming. That list is the
# rate limit, the determinism story and the debugging story all at once,
# and none of it needed inventing -- it is what being an event already
# meant.
#
# WHAT A SWAP DOES TO A SOUNDING NOTE: it cuts it. The replacement is
# built off the audio thread and published at a window boundary, and
# publishing it *replaces* the channel -- so the old graph's voices stop
# right there, with no release. Loading a patch has always done this; the
# promise it keeps is that the outgoing channel is not freed under the
# audio thread, which is a promise about lifetimes and not about notes.
#
# Which is why `swapping' runs on a forty-second clock and `line' plays
# short phrases: a swap wants to land in a gap. The fine edit below has
# no such problem -- a node-arg change touches the prototype tree, so
# whatever is sounding stays sounding and the next voice is the new one.
#
# `reshape' IS THE LINE BEING CROSSED, and it is worth reading slowly.
# Every composer before this one was held to a patch's declared chanargs
# -- COMPOSITION_HANDOFF.md section 9 argued at length that the limit
# was encapsulation rather than a missing feature. `reshape' names a
# node in the .dsp and one of *its* args: `fmap.inmax' is not a chanarg,
# was never offered, and until phase 4 nothing in the composer world
# could have said its name. Section 9 also said the way past would be a
# different mechanism rather than a widening of the old one, and this is
# it. The consent moved rather than vanished: a piece reaching this deep
# has said so in a line anyone can read, in a file they can diff.
#
# Channels: the loader hands one to each declared instrument, so `voice'
# is on the first free one and `bell' and `glass' sit on their own until
# a swap moves one of them over. The sink names `voice', which means
# "the channel voice lives on" -- the slot, not the graph in it.

name "Reshape";
author "Misha Nasledov";
description "A piece that rebuilds its own instrument while it plays.";

seed 8021;

scale emin "E2 G2 B2 E3 F#3 G3 B3 D4 E4";

# Three instruments, three characters, one slot. `voice' is where the
# notes go; the other two are what the slot can become.
instrument voice {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";
    a    = 500 ms;
    d    = 1200 ms;
    s    = 0.75;
    r    = 2600 ms;
    res  = 0.6;
    fmin = 0.08;
    fmax = 0.55;
};

instrument bell {
    dsp  "amb01.dsp";
    a    = 4 ms;
    d    = 2600 ms;
    s    = 0.05;
    r    = 2600 ms;
    res  = 0.9;
    fmin = 0.2;
    fmax = 0.95;
};

instrument glass {
    dsp  "ts1.dsp";
    a    = 40 ms;
    d    = 900 ms;
    s    = 0.4;
    r    = 1800 ms;
    cutoff = 9;
    res    = 0.5;
};

# The notes. They know nothing about any of this and never change --
# every difference you hear over the piece is the instrument underneath
# them being rebuilt.
chain line {
    stage src gen::eno_line {
        notes = emin;  period = 3.9 s;  jitter = 1.1 s;
        prob = 0.8;  hold = 6 s;  vel = 66;  vel_jitter = 8;
    };
    sink { instrument = voice; };
};

chain under {
    stage src gen::eno_line {
        notes = "E2 B2";  period = 9.7 s;  jitter = 2.5 s;
        prob = 0.6;  hold = 11 s;  vel = 52;  vel_jitter = 6;
    };
    sink { instrument = voice; };
};

# The coarse edit. Round-robin so the piece has a shape you can learn
# rather than one you have to wait out; `order = 1' picks from the seed
# instead, and still never picks what is already sounding.
chain swapping {
    stage m gen::swap {
        instruments = "voice,bell,glass";
        every = 40 s;
        order = 0;
    };
    sink { instrument = voice; };
};

# The fine edit, and the one that reaches past what any patch declared.
#
# `fmap' is the node inside amb01.dsp that maps a note's velocity onto
# the filter's range, and `inmax' is the top of the velocity it expects.
# Lower it and a soft note opens the filter as far as a hard one did --
# the instrument stops caring how hard it is played. amb01 exposes res,
# fmin, fmax, the envelope and the three detunes as chanargs; it does
# not expose this, and it never meant to. The sweep moves the whole
# piece between velocity-sensitive and flat over about a minute and a
# half, and back.
#
# It is aimed at the *slot*, so when the swap puts ts1.dsp there the node
# is gone and the host says so once per step rather than pretending it
# worked. That is the honest behaviour and it is worth seeing: a
# structure edit names something in a graph, and graphs change underneath
# it. Nothing else in the piece stops.
chain sensitivity {
    stage r gen::reshape {
        node = "fmap";  arg = "inmax";
        from = 1;  to = 0.25;
        every = 11 s;  steps = 9;
    };
    sink { instrument = voice; };
};
